export const NGO_SYMMETRY: FleetText[] = [
  { domain: "darpan-welfare-join", text: "Aligned set (Ekal Abhiyan, Vivekananda Kendra, DRI, Akshaya Patra/ISKCON) vs neutral/critical set (Sulabh; the Right-to-Food SHG suppliers; and, from the sibling files, Oxfam/CARE/CPR): for ministry grants-in-aid the same fields are empty on both sides because no reachable page publishes recipients — the honest result is 'not testable here', not 'clean'. Where a recipient-level Union payment is documented it is the Gandhi Peace Prize purse: 3 of 5 laureates in the 2015–18 batch are Sangh/ISKCON-affiliated, chosen unanimously by a jury including the CJI and the Congress leader; Sulabh got the same ₹1 cr. The largest documented state flows to any association here go to Akshaya Patra (₹205 cr FY18, ₹72 cr from Karnataka), which is FCRA-registered and religiously run — and the same Karnataka government that fought it over onions renewed its MoU. On the delivery side the lens is symmetric across parties: SHG suppliers were displaced by big contractors under Maharashtra's BJP-led government (2016–17) and paid irregularly under MP's BJP government (2018–24), while the PUCL rule they were breaching dates from UPA-era litigation; the same SHG network is the intake layer for Shinde/BJP's Ladki Bahin and the payment layer for Nitish Kumar's MMRY. Nothing in this file distinguishes the aligned from the critical associations except the absence of data." },
  { domain: "donors-narratives", text: "Same lens on the controls. (1) Domestic philanthropy: Tata Trusts, Azim Premji Foundation, Reliance Foundation and Adani Foundation face no prior-permission gate, no watch list and no donor-wise disclosure of the FCRA kind; Bain puts private giving at ~₹1.31 lakh crore in FY2024 against a foreign-contribution channel that is a small fraction of it — so the regulatory attention is inversely proportional to scale. Their pages were not all retrievable, so their individual disbursements are a gap. (2) Government-aligned foreign donors: Sewa International USA and IDRF are documented (by their critics' reading of 990s) as remitting to Sangh-affiliated service networks, exactly as Ford, OSF and Christian charities remit to their grantees; the state acted against Ford (2015–16), OSF (2016→, 2025) and Compassion (2017) and has no recorded action against the Sangh-linked channel. Both sides' 'political intent' claims rest on inference from grantee ideology; neither has an adjudicated finding. (3) The Government itself: USAID's documented India money went to seven Union projects (US$97m FY24) — orders of magnitude more than any documented USAID money to Indian associations — and nobody called it interference. (4) The Gates Foundation, the largest foreign health donor with formal state MoUs and an award to the PM, has no recorded MHA action; the one large foreign donor treated as a partner is the one that does not fund rights advocacy. Finding about the lens: 'foreign money → foreign influence' produces an alarming picture of every side, including the state; what varies is whom the state chooses to apply it to." },
  { domain: "fcra-actions", text: "UPA (2011–14) against NDA (2014–26): the UPA-era record reachable here shows the same instrument used the same way — INSAF suspended 2013-04-30 on an onward-transfer-for-protests ground and quashed as premature and unreasoned; CPSC/People's Watch suspended three times 2012–14 and vindicated in 2014-03; three Kudankulam-area associations cancelled 2012-02 with the Prime Minister naming US and Scandinavian funders; a weakly-sourced 4,141 cancellations in 2012-13. The NDA-era record adds scale (20,600 cancellations by 2021, mostly non-filing; 5,933 cessations on one day in 2022), the 2020 statute, and a longer list of named critics — but the same lens on non-critics produces Missionaries of Charity (refused, restored), World Vision (₹170 crore), CASA, EFI, Compassion (US$45m/yr), CARE India (₹377.5 crore), Bal Raksha Bharat (₹163 crore), VHAI, Hemophilia Federation, Institute of Economic Growth. Government-aligned associations (VIF, India Foundation, Ekal/Vidya Bharati affiliates) were not examined in this file — they belong to the receipts agent; the absence of any recorded enforcement against them is a question for that file, not a finding of this one. Courts: under both governments the High Courts struck MHA on procedure (2013, 2014, 2015); after the 2020 Amendment they deferred (CHRI 2022; Noel Harper 2022)." },
  { domain: "fcra-receipts", text: "Government-critical (Oxfam India, CPR, CARE) and non-critical/aligned (World Vision India, Compassion partners, Believers/Ayana, RDT) recipients were recorded with the same fields: receipts where found, enforcement event, stated ground, response. Result: the largest enforcement events by money fell on service charities that do not criticise the government (Compassion ~₹325 cr/yr; Ayana ₹826 cr; WVI ₹170–319 cr; RDT ₹209 cr), while the critic cases (Oxfam, CPR) carry stated grounds tied to content — the lens produces an alarming picture on BOTH sides, which is a finding about the regime's breadth rather than about targeting. UPA-era control: the Dec 2013 Parliament answer shows the same ministry, same statute, 41,844 registrants with 40% non-filers and ₹11,548 cr receipts — the non-filing problem that drove 91% of later cancellations predates 2014. Aligned-side receipts (Ekal, Sewa, VIF, India Foundation) could not be found at all, so the symmetry on receipts is incomplete and recorded as a void. Domestic control: Azim Premji Foundation alone disburses ₹1,639 cr/yr (7% of all FC), and Bain/Dasra put foreign private giving (~₹23,000 cr) at ~19% of private philanthropy and ~1% of total social-sector spend — foreign money is a minor stream in Indian welfare finance." },
  { domain: "political-trusts", text: "Applying the same lens (documented FCRA enforcement action, or a documented foreign-money controversy, tied to a national party's central leadership or its aligned think tanks) produced a strikingly asymmetric result in what we could actually find and verify this session — but the asymmetry is partly an artifact of press coverage and partly an artifact of this session's access limits, and one important sub-question is symmetric on the primary evidence we DID verify. On the Congress side: RGF and RGCT were both investigated from 2020 and had their FCRA registrations cancelled in Oct 2022 on stated grounds including a Chinese-embassy donation and a Zakir Naik/IRF donation (detailed in this directory's fcra-actions.json); RGICS and the Indira Gandhi Memorial Trust were named in the same 2020 probe. On the BJP/RSS side, applying the identical search — did VIF, India Foundation, Prajna Pravah or OFBJP-USA face any documented FCRA cancellation, suspension or comparable enforcement action — found NOTHING in any source we could open this session (see voids). That zero could mean these organisations are in fact compliant and never triggered scrutiny, or it could mean our access to the Indian press archive was badly degraded this session (see gaps: most major outlets' search functions returned 403s, JS-only shells, or unrelated fallback content for every guessed URL) — we cannot distinguish these two explanations from what we found, and it would be wrong to present the zero as proof of a double standard. Where we DID verify a genuinely symmetric documented fact — the 2014 Delhi High Court's finding that BOTH BJP and Congress received unlawful foreign-source donations via Vedanta subsidiaries, and the subsequent 2016/2018 retrospective amendments reported to have legalised this for both parties identically — the record is symmetric on its face: same violation, same two respondents, same legislative fix applied to both. That is the one part of this file's brief we can say, with real confidence, treated both parties identically." },
];

