        "denialFound": "HVK / Ashok Chowgule rebuttal, opened at https://hvk.org/specialreports/hate/1.html. Its §1.1 summarises the report as saying \"because IDRF provides donations to some of the social service projects started by the Hindutva organisations, IDRF is funding the violence\". It attacks the authors' agenda (\"conclude first and then get the data\"), \"guilt by association\" and \"perverting the data\" (§1.5). It does not dispute that money flows to Sangh-run service projects. What it disputes is the characterisation and the method. IDRF's own November 2002 \"pure concoction\" statement and the 2003 six-academic rebuttal (letindiadevelop.org returns 403) were not opened. WebSearch was exhausted this session, and idrf.org's current site shows nothing about the report. No evidence shows whether IDRF was asked before publication.",
        "innocentReading": "IDRF's founders and officers had Sangh links, and in rural and tribal India the largest Hindu-oriented networks that run schools and tribal welfare (VKA, Sewa Bharati, Ekal) are Sangh affiliates. A diaspora charity picking its own grantees would send money to networks it knows and trusts. On the report's own figures, about 70% of IDRF funds went to education and about 15% to relief. Both ends of the flow were lawful and declared, and no tribunal has found that IDRF money paid for violence. The \"Sangh-affiliated\" coding is the critics' own classification.",
        "reason": "The cited source was opened and it supports the core edge as an allegation by a named party: \"A vast majority (in excess of 80%) of the IDRF designated funds were sent to Parivar organizations, especially those affiliated with or controlled by the RSS, the VHP and the Vanvasi Kalyan Ashram (VKA).\" Alleged is the right tier. The only primary is the critics' report, the rebuttal disputes its coding, and IDRF's schedules have not been opened. A contra (c024) exists, as the tier requires. The HVK rebuttal concedes that the flow exists and contests the characterisation. The edge is not refuted, but the claim text contains a false caveat. It says the \"83%\" figure is \"from secondary summaries, not verified in the primary text\". The primary text at the cited URL states it: \"None of the organizations funded can be identified with any minority community, though 8% (in addition to the 83% that are Sangh affiliated) are clearly identifiable as Hindu or Jain religious organizations.\" The d field's \"Amount not stated in the section opened\" is also wrong, because the section states \"over $ 3.2 million\". The claim also overstates scope. The 80–83% applies to IDRF-designated funds, about 75% of disbursements. Donor-designated funds were 10% Sangh. Combining the two gives roughly 65% of total disbursements. That still counts as \"predominantly\", but as the claim's own inference, not the source's sentence.",
