        "denialFound": "None found from Sewa International USA. ThePrint says: \"ThePrint has reached out to the organizations mentioned in this article for their comments on the report.\" Sewa is mentioned in the article, so it was presumably asked, but no Sewa reply was published. HAF (\"All the allegations [in this report] about HAF are baseless\") and HSS (\"a politically motivated smear campaign\") replied for themselves only. Sewa's About page calls it \"a Hindu faith-based, humanitarian, nonprofit service organization\" that \"serves humanity irrespective of race, color, religion\", but it does not address the report.",
        "innocentReading": "The report's Sewa figure comes from Form 990 Part III row 4e (2008–2019), which is total program service expenses. That covers Sewa's US work (disaster relief such as the Harvey response, refugee and Covid programmes) as well as work abroad, and it is not a record of grants to India. The report admits this: \"a group did not appear to differentiate consistently between programs in the U.S. from those in India, and further investigation is needed to disaggregate the funding.\" ProPublica totals for Sewa's expenses in 2014–2018 come to about $9.7m, in line with the report's $12.9m for 2014–19. That suggests the $17.1m is close to all of Sewa's spending everywhere, not money sent to India. The report's own author also says \"most of [these actions] are legal\". The boring explanation is that this is a US Hindu faith-based relief charity with some Indian partners, and board members with HSS roles.",
