        "denialFound": "A government-side ground is on record, and the claim leaves it out. A senior MHA official told India Today (9 Dec 2016): \"We are ready to reconsider the case of Compassion International, the US donor, which was put under 'prior permission category' for alleged violation of Foreign Contribution Regulation Act (FCRA)\". The article adds that Compassion \"is accused of funding non-Foreign Contribution Regulation Act registered NGOs\" (https://www.indiatoday.in/india/delhi/story/us-ngo-compassion-international-foreign-contribution-regulation-act-356547-2016-12-09). The MEA spokesperson, also to India Today and quoted in CT, said \"India welcomes lawful operation of foreign NGOs in India\" and called the hearing \"based on limited understanding of India\". Compassion's position is in the testimony: no explanation was ever given, and the renewal refusals cited only \"On the basis of Field Agency Report\".",
        "innocentReading": "The government's own account is a compliance problem, not an ideological one. MHA says Compassion was listed because it allegedly sent money to Indian associations that were not registered under FCRA, which the Act restricts. There is some support for this in Compassion's own history. It worked through thousands of partner churches, and its partner CBV had a long-running income-tax dispute (from 2011–13) over transfers to religious-registered charities. The 'national interest' wording belongs to the batch of 25 renewal refusals, not to this listing. Neither the compliance account nor Compassion's discrimination account has been tested in a published order or a court ruling.",
