        "sourceCheck": "partially",
        "denialFound": "No denial of the listing itself was found. Congress's response to the June 2020 allegation (Hindustan Times, 2020-07-08, https://www.hindustantimes.com/india-news/home-ministry-sets-up-panel-to-coordinate-probe-into-3-gandhi-family-trusts/story-KpJkxlJqXIUUhLVwzvq2oN.html) was that \"the BJP was engaging in diversionary tactics\". It also \"questioned it on visits and exchanges between the BJP and the Communist Party of China (CPC)\". On the 2022 cancellation, Abhishek Manu Singhvi said \"The Rajiv Gandhi Foundation has nothing to add, nothing to fear... you don't ask these questions of many holy cows\" (reported via Indian Express / indiacsr.in). No statement from RGF itself about the embassy line-item was found. Whether RGF was asked is unknown.",
        "innocentReading": "RGF (and RGICS) ran a study of an India-China free trade arrangement during a period of officially promoted India-China rapprochement under the 2005 Strategic and Cooperative Partnership and the 2006 Friendship Year. The embassy partnered in or co-funded that dialogue work, as foreign missions commonly do with policy institutes. RGF then published the embassy's name in its own annual report. Voluntary publication is the opposite of concealment, and it is the only reason the BJP could cite it in 2020. Whether the receipt breached the 1976 Act's procedural requirements (prior permission or returns) is a separate, still-unadjudicated question. It is tiered alleged in fcra-actions:c057.",
